#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069964 :tutorial/content "When solving an equation, we sometimes require taking the square root of a negative number. In such cases, we will not end up with real solutions, but imaginary solutions.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's solve the equation\n\n$$\n2x^2 +8 = 0\n$$\n\nfor $x$. First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2x^2 + 8 &= 0 \\\\[3pt] 2x^2 &= -8 \\\\[3pt] x^2 &= -4 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy taking the square root, we obtain\n\n$$\nx = \\pm \\sqrt{-4}\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $x$ using the imaginary unit\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\,\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= ± \\sqrt{-4} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{4 \\cdot (-1)} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{4} \\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\\n&= ± 2\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx = \\pm 2 \\textrm{i}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5649dc90-3e67-5125-9612-6e0618aea772" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2786 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
